.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt PIPE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pipe ,
.Nm pipe2
.Nd create an anonymous pipe
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn pipe "int pipefd[2]"
.Ft int
.Fn pipe2 "int pipefd[2]" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn pipe
creates a pipe and stores its read descriptor in
.Fa pipefd Ns [0]
and its write descriptor in
.Fa pipefd Ns [1] .
The pipe has a 4096-byte buffer, which
.Dv F_GETPIPE_SZ
of
.Xr fcntl 2
reports.
Bytes are read in the order written.
.Pp
.Fn pipe2
does the same, with flags applied to both ends.
The permitted flags, declared in
.In fcntl.h ,
are:
.Bl -tag -width "O_NONBLOCK"
.It Dv O_NONBLOCK
Set nonblocking I/O on both open-file descriptions.
An empty read with a writer still open returns
.Er EAGAIN ;
a write to a full pipe returns
.Er EAGAIN
if no bytes have been written.
.It Dv O_CLOEXEC
Set the close-on-exec flag on both descriptors.
A successful
.Xr execve 2
closes them.
.El
.Pp
Other flag bits are refused.
.Fn pipe
is equivalent to
.Fn pipe2
with flags zero.
.Pp
Without
.Dv O_NONBLOCK ,
reads wait while the buffer is empty and a writer remains open;
writes wait while it is full and a reader remains open.
These waits allow other processes to run.
A signal interrupting a wait produces
.Er EINTR
if no bytes have been transferred, and the call is restarted instead, as
in Linux, when the signal runs no handler or its handler has
.Dv SA_RESTART .
A read returns at most the bytes available when its first transfer starts,
up to the requested count.
A write that stops after transferring bytes returns that count.
.Pp
The kernel resolves user buffers one page at a time before copying bytes.
Resolution can wait for memory reclaim and allow another reader or writer
to run; the pipe state is checked again afterwards.
If another reader drains the pipe after this call has copied some bytes,
the call returns that partial count without waiting for more data.
A user-buffer fault returns the count already transferred, or
.Er EFAULT
if no bytes have been transferred.
.Pp
Once every write descriptor has closed, reads consume the remaining
buffered bytes and then return 0 for end of file.
A nonempty write after every read descriptor has closed posts
.Dv SIGPIPE
to the writing thread and returns
.Er EPIPE
if no bytes have been written.
If the reader disappears after some bytes have been written, the
write posts the signal and returns the byte count.
.Pp
Descriptors duplicated or inherited across
.Xr fork 2
share their end's open-file description.
The end closes only when its last descriptor closes.
The pipe is freed when both ends have closed.
.Pp
The i386 system call numbers are 42 for
.Fn pipe
and 331 for
.Fn pipe2 .
EBX points to the two-element descriptor array;
.Fn pipe2
takes its flags in ECX.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success, or -1 on failure and set
.Va errno .
The kernel interface returns the negative error number.
Failure releases any pipe and descriptors allocated by the call.
The descriptors are reserved and given their ends only after both
numbers have been stored in
.Fa pipefd ,
so another thread sharing the descriptor table cannot use or duplicate
either before the call returns.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/pipeprobe.c
writes ten bytes, reads them back in order, closes the writer, and
checks for end of file.
It also fills a nonblocking pipe and checks
.Er EAGAIN ,
then closes a pipe's reader and checks
.Er EPIPE
and delivery of
.Dv SIGPIPE .
.Sh ERRORS
Creation can fail with:
.Bl -tag -width "[EMFILE]"
.It Bq Er EFAULT
The two descriptors cannot be copied to user memory.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn pipe2
was given a flag other than
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
or
.Dv O_CLOEXEC .
.It Bq Er EMFILE
Two descriptors are not free below the process's descriptor limit.
.It Bq Er ENFILE
The kernel heap cannot allocate the pipe.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The kernel heap has no room for a larger descriptor table or for an
open-file description.
.It Bq Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr close 2 ,
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr read 2 ,
.Xr write 2
